 ## Overview
-Scene Flow Regularization is a technique used in dynamic radiance field optimization to model 3D temporal motion by learning mappings between temporally neighboring time steps [S1, unofficial][S2]. It solves the problem of inconsistent geometry and appearance in novel view synthesis of dynamic scenes by enforcing physical plausibility on the estimated 3D displacements [S1, unofficial]. Unlike static regularization, it explicitly predicts 3D vectors for every point, setting it apart from methods that rely solely on 2D optical flow or implicit deformation fields without explicit flow supervision [S1, unofficial][S2].
+Scene Flow Regularization is a technique used in dynamic radiance field optimization to model 3D temporal motion by learning mappings between temporally neighboring time steps [S1, unofficial][S2]. It addresses inconsistent geometry and appearance in dynamic novel view synthesis by explicitly predicting 3D displacement vectors for every point and regularizing them directly [S1, unofficial][S2]. Unlike methods relying solely on 2D optical flow or implicit deformation fields, it provides explicit 3D flow supervision and enforces smoothness constraints on the predicted motion [S1, unofficial][S2].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input:** 3D coordinates $(x, y, z)$ and a timestamp $t_i$.
+*   **Input:** 3D coordinates $(x, y, z)$ and a timestamp $t_i$ [S2].
 *   **Output:** Two 3D scene flow vectors: $S_{i \rightarrow i+1}$ (displacement to the next frame) and $S_{i \rightarrow i-1}$ (displacement to the previous frame) [S2].
 
 **Data Structures**
-*   **Scene Flow MLP:** A multi-layer perceptron $\text{MLP}_{\theta_{sf}}$ that takes $(x, y, z, t_i)$ as input and outputs the concatenated flow vectors [S2].
-    *   *Note:* The scene flow MLP is not part of the rendering process but is part of the loss computation [S2].
